WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.615
You have just installed a tool.

2
00:00:04.615 --> 00:00:07.828
Homebrew, an app that installs other tools on a Mac.

3
00:00:07.828 --> 00:00:11.659
The installer prints a long wall of text that ends in success.

4
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:14.582
Feeling good, you type brew --version.

5
00:00:14.582 --> 00:00:17.512
And the terminal says: command not found.

6
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:19.493
So, did the install fail?

7
00:00:19.493 --> 00:00:21.534
Should you download it again?

8
00:00:21.534 --> 00:00:22.660
Restart the Mac?

9
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:23.779
None of those.

10
00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:26.058
The tool is on your Mac, right now.

11
00:00:26.058 --> 00:00:30.161
To see why the terminal can't find it, let's go to the kitchen.

12
00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:35.200
Last night the flour arrived, and you put it in a cupboard by the back door.

13
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.316
In the morning, you go looking for it.

14
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:40.847
You check your usual shelves, one by one, from the top.

15
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:42.576
Not on the top shelf.

16
00:00:42.576 --> 00:00:44.156
Not on the middle one.

17
00:00:44.156 --> 00:00:45.737
Not on the bottom one.

18
00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:48.361
So you announce: we have no flour.

19
00:00:48.361 --> 00:00:49.135
But you do.

20
00:00:49.135 --> 00:00:50.614
It is in the kitchen.

21
00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:53.289
It is just not on any shelf you check.

22
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.794
Your Mac does exactly this.

23
00:00:55.794 --> 00:01:01.307
The program inside the terminal window that runs what you type is called the shell.

24
00:01:01.307 --> 00:01:02.569
On a Mac it is zsh.

25
00:01:02.569 --> 00:01:05.227
The shell is you, looking for the flour.

26
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:07.813
The shelves are folders on your disk.

27
00:01:07.813 --> 00:01:12.764
A command, like ls or git, is a program file sitting in one of those folders.

28
00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:16.598
When you type a name, the shell does not search your whole Mac.

29
00:01:16.598 --> 00:01:21.151
It checks a short list of folders, in order, and runs the first match it finds.

30
00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:24.075
And the cupboard by the back door?

31
00:01:24.075 --> 00:01:31.961
On Apple Silicon Macs, Homebrew lives in /opt/homebrew, and puts its commands in /opt/homebrew/bin.

32
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.008
Watch the shell look for brew.

33
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.410
The list ends, so it gives up.

34
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:37.616
Command not found.

35
00:01:37.616 --> 00:01:41.972
The list macOS starts you with does not include Homebrew's shelf.

36
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.619
That list of shelves has a name.

37
00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:46.069
It is called your PATH.

38
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.189
So command not found is a report about the list, not about your disk.

39
00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:52.500
The shell is telling you where it looked.

40
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:54.682
Here is the step everyone skips.

41
00:01:54.682 --> 00:02:00.405
When the Homebrew installer finishes, it prints Next steps, with two or three commands to run.

42
00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:06.527
It is easy to miss, because they come right after a wall of text that looks like the job is done.

43
00:02:06.527 --> 00:02:08.775
An installer that ends with homework.

44
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.498
So the point is: read the last lines of any installer.

45
00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:14.636
Run exactly what this one prints.

46
00:02:14.636 --> 00:02:17.681
Those lines add Homebrew's folder to your PATH.

47
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:24.354
They also save the change in ~/.zprofile, a file each new shell reads when it starts.

48
00:02:24.354 --> 00:02:26.404
So open a new terminal tab.

49
00:02:26.633 --> 00:02:27.710
Type brew again.

50
00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:31.681
The search reaches Homebrew's shelf, finds brew, and stops.

51
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.448
When a tool is installed but not found, check PATH first.

52
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:39.178
Homebrew's shelf holds more than brew.

53
00:02:39.178 --> 00:02:46.548
This course installs python@3.14 there, with pip3.14, the tool that installs Python packages.

54
00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:48.658
Now a second kitchen problem.

55
00:02:48.658 --> 00:02:53.613
A baker who makes rye and white bread keeps a separate starter jar for each.

56
00:02:53.613 --> 00:02:57.069
Share one jar, and the rye ends up in the white loaf.

57
00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:05.541
Python projects have the same risk, because two projects may want different versions of the same package, the add-on code they use.

58
00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:12.422
So each project gets a virtual environment, or venv: a private folder of packages for one project.

59
00:03:12.422 --> 00:03:18.601
It has its own bin shelf, with its own python, and its own pip, Python's package installer.

60
00:03:18.833 --> 00:03:23.000
You switch it on with source .venv/bin/activate.

61
00:03:23.233 --> 00:03:24.558
So here is a question.

62
00:03:24.558 --> 00:03:27.869
Where on the list does activating put the venv's shelf?

63
00:03:27.869 --> 00:03:30.699
And then, when you type python, which one runs?

64
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:40.093
Activating puts the venv's shelf first on your PATH, and your prompt starts with (.venv).

65
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.751
So python and pip now mean the copies inside the venv.

66
00:03:43.751 --> 00:03:46.372
The search stops at the very first shelf.

67
00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.455
This project's packages can't clash with another project's.

68
00:03:50.455 --> 00:03:53.525
First match wins, and the first shelf is yours.

69
00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.030
Now the trap.

70
00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:00.269
Tomorrow you open a new terminal, forget to activate, and install a package.

71
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.707
This time the search finds pip on Homebrew's shelf.

72
00:04:03.707 --> 00:04:08.172
And Homebrew's own Python refuses, with externally-managed-environment.

73
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:08.772
Well.

74
00:04:08.772 --> 00:04:12.264
That is a very long way of saying: wrong shelf.

75
00:04:12.264 --> 00:04:14.195
The error means one thing.

76
00:04:14.195 --> 00:04:15.979
Your venv is not active.

77
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:23.842
And the tempting shortcut, sudo pip with no venv, only makes packages from different projects fight over versions.

78
00:04:23.842 --> 00:04:26.389
Homebrew's Python refuses that anyway.

79
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:31.627
So before any pip install, check that your prompt starts with (.venv).

80
00:04:31.627 --> 00:04:33.781
If it doesn't, activate first.

81
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.148
So, the moral of the story.

82
00:04:36.148 --> 00:04:38.589
The shell never searches your whole Mac.

83
00:04:38.589 --> 00:04:42.861
It checks a short list of shelves, in order, and runs the first match.

84
00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:46.013
Homebrew's Next steps put its shelf on the list.

85
00:04:46.013 --> 00:04:49.267
And a venv puts its own shelf first, for one project.

86
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.196
A quick check.

87
00:04:51.196 --> 00:04:57.386
You open a new terminal in your hello-devops folder, and pytest says command not found.

88
00:04:57.386 --> 00:04:58.809
It worked yesterday.

89
00:04:58.809 --> 00:04:59.805
What is wrong?

90
00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:08.563
pytest lives on the venv's shelf, and every new terminal starts without the venv active.

91
00:05:08.563 --> 00:05:11.516
Activate it, and the shelf is back on the list.
